{#
  Slide-out drawer macro — modal dialog overlay (tasks.md P18-T6).

  HTML tag: <dialog>
  CSS class: slide-out-drawer

  Accessibility:
    - A native <dialog> (role="dialog" implicitly) with aria-modal="true". The claim is true only when the
      consumer opens it with `el.showModal()`: the browser then puts it in the top layer, makes the page
      behind it inert, traps Tab inside, moves focus in, closes it on Escape (the cancel event) and returns
      focus to the opener on `el.close()`. Never open it by adding the `open` attribute: that shows it
      non-modally while it still says aria-modal.
    - A closed <dialog> is not rendered, so the macro sets no `hidden`.

  Params: label (required), id, classes, attributes.
  The label has no default (no English from us: the consumer names it in their language). `open` is
  ignored: a modal cannot be opened from markup, so call `showModal()`. Caller provides content.

  Usage:
    {% call slideOutDrawer({ id: "nav", label: "Navigation" }) %}
      <nav>…</nav>
      <form method="dialog"><button>Close</button></form>
    {% endcall %}
    <script>document.getElementById("nav").showModal();</script>
#}
{%- macro slideOutDrawer(params) -%}
{%- set p = params | default({}) -%}
<dialog
  class="slide-out-drawer{% if p.classes %} {{ p.classes }}{% endif %}"
  aria-modal="true"
  tabindex="-1"
  {%- if p.label %} aria-label="{{ p.label }}"{% endif %}
  {%- if p.id %} id="{{ p.id }}"{% endif %}
  {%- if p.attributes %}{% for k, v in p.attributes %} {{ k }}="{{ v }}"{% endfor %}{% endif %}
>
  {%- if p.html -%}{{ p.html | safe }}
  {%- elif caller -%}{{ caller() }}
  {%- endif -%}
</dialog>
{%- endmacro -%}
